<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<chapter xml:id="ch7-chapter-bootstrap" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>The Bootstrap</title>

  <introduction>
    <p> Students have been learning how important it is to obtain a measure of sampling variability when making decisions about hypothesis tests. 
      So far we have simulated statistics for hypothetical situations only (null hypothesis) using cards, coin flips, spinners, etc. 
      This chapter introduces bootstrapping for the estimation of sampling variability. Students will learn how to simulate the random sampling process when we only have access to sample data and not the full population.
      Students will also begin to learn about parameter estimation. 
    </p>
  </introduction>


  <objectives>
    <ol>
    <li>Explore...</li>

    <li>Learn...</li>

    <li>Understand...</li>

    <li>Apply...</li>
  </ol>
  </objectives>

  

